            <ArticleTitle>Lipofibromatous Hamartoma of the Median Nerve: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3941/jrcr.v10i11.2787</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Lipofibromatous hamartoma is a rare and slow growing benign fibro-fatty tumor. It is characterized by the proliferation of mature adipocytes within the epineurium and the perineurium of the peripheral nerves. In the upper extremity, it most commonly affects the median nerve. Median nerve involvement commonly leads to pain, numbness, paresthesia and carpal tunnel syndrome. This article presents a case of lipofibromatous hamartoma in an 8-year-old child followed by a discussion of the epidemiology, diagnosis, imaging details and treatment options for this condition.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
